<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#japanese-tea-ceremony">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Japanese tea ceremony</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as tea ceremony</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> ceremony is a ritual preparation and serving of powdered green tea, shaped by Zen Buddhist ideals of simplicity and harmony. It was refined by masters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu">Sen no Rikyū</a> in the sixteenth century. It flourished under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> and is part of its cultural legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony.jpg" alt="Japanese tea ceremony" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stéphane D'Alu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tea_ceremony_performing_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first documented evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> dates to the 9th century. An entry in the Nihon Kōki describes the Buddhist monk Eichū bringing tea back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, and in 815 he personally served sencha to Emperor Saga on an excursion at Karasaki. By imperial order in 816, tea plantations began to be cultivated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinai">Kinki region</a>, though interest in tea then faded. Buddhists used tea to prevent drowsiness during long meditations.</p><p>In the 1500s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu">Sen no Rikyū</a> revolutionised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> culture. He redefined the rules of the tea house, tea garden, utensils and procedures, introduced a much smaller chashitsu and rustic, distorted ceramic tea bowls, and based the ceremony on the aesthetic of wabi. His great-grandchildren founded the Omotesenke, Urasenke and Mushakōjisenke schools, and the practice spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> to the general public. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Zen Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> also shaped it: washing the hands and mouth before the ceremony follows the Shinto purification ritual of misogi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The ceremony is known as sadō or chadō, the way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, or chanoyu, and centres on the presentation of powdered green tea, matcha, a procedure called temae. Koicha, thick tea, uses about three times as much tea to the same water as usucha, thin tea, which is whipped with a bamboo whisk. A relatively simple gathering, a chakai, includes confections, thin tea and perhaps a light meal. A formal chaji usually includes a full kaiseki meal, then confections, thick tea and thin tea, and may last up to four hours.</p><p>The year is divided into the sunken hearth season, traditionally November to April, and the brazier season, May to October. Rooms are ideally tatami-floored, with a hanging scroll in the tokonoma alcove chosen for the season and occasion, and a simple flower arrangement, chabana, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu">Sen no Rikyū</a> developed. Most ceremonies are conducted in kimono, whose sleeves and obi shape many of the movements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Schools teach many styles of temae. Obon temae, a simple procedure for thin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> on a tray, is usually the first learned. Ryūrei, with host and guests seated on chairs, originated in the Urasenke school, initially for non-Japanese guests, who, it was thought, would be more comfortable that way. Ten artisans hold the title Senke Jusshoku for supplying utensils to the three main schools.</p><p>The English term Teaism was coined by Okakura Kakuzō to describe the worldview of the way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, not only its presentation. A separate tradition, senchadō, uses leaf tea and was introduced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 17th century by Ingen, founder of the Ōbaku school of Zen, and popularised in the 18th century by the Ōbaku monk Baisao, who sold tea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Japanese tea ceremony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>tea ceremony</b> moved from a large open room to a small, quiet setting and became a form of meditation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Older arts such as the <b>tea ceremony</b> and garden design were further refined and widely disseminated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
